<PageLayout
  title="Presbyterian Church Maui | [CHURCH NAME]"
  description="Looking for a Presbyterian church on Maui? We're planting one — elder-led, confessional, part of the Orthodox Presbyterian Church."
  canonical="https://reformedchurchmaui.com/presbyterian-church-maui/"
  h1="Presbyterian Church on Maui, Hawaii"
  breadcrumb={[{ name: 'Presbyterian Church Maui', url: 'https://reformedchurchmaui.com/presbyterian-church-maui/' }]}
>
  <p class="text-[#5F5E5A] text-base leading-relaxed mb-10">
    If you've been searching for a Presbyterian church on Maui, here's what that word actually means — and what we're building.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-[#1E2A3A] mt-10 mb-4">What makes a church "Presbyterian"?</h2>
  <p class="text-[#5F5E5A] leading-relaxed mb-8">
    "Presbyterian" describes how a church is governed, not just what it believes. Presbyterian churches are led by a session of ordained elders rather than a single pastor or a congregational vote on every decision. Elders are chosen from within the congregation to give oversight, provide pastoral care, and hold one another accountable. Local churches are also connectional — accountable to a broader assembly (a presbytery) of elders from other congregations, which protects against any one church or leader operating without oversight.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-[#1E2A3A] mt-10 mb-4">Are all Presbyterian churches the same?</h2>
  <p class="text-[#5F5E5A] leading-relaxed mb-8">
    Not exactly. The OPC and PCA hold to the historic Westminster Standards; the PCUSA has departed from them on a number of doctrinal questions and we are not affiliated with it. For the full comparison of how OPC relates to PCA, PCUSA, and other traditions, see <a href="/what-we-believe/" class="text-[#C4A35A] hover:underline">What We Believe</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-[#1E2A3A] mt-10 mb-4">What to expect as a member</h2>
  <p class="text-[#5F5E5A] leading-relaxed mb-8">
    Being part of a Presbyterian church means real accountability in both directions — elders who know you and care for you personally, and a congregation that has a voice through its session. New members typically meet with the elders before joining, not as a formality but so the church actually knows who's part of it. It's a smaller, more personal model than a large elder board overseeing a crowd.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-[#1E2A3A] mt-10 mb-4">Planting a Presbyterian church on Maui</h2>
  <p class="text-[#5F5E5A] leading-relaxed mb-12">
    We are currently the only confessional Presbyterian church plant on Maui. For the full story of why that gap exists and what we're building to fill it, see <a href="/reformed-church-maui/" class="text-[#C4A35A] hover:underline">Reformed Church Maui</a>.
  </p>

